## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of privacy-preserving distributed learning and the real-world trade-offs of deploying it at scale, including regulatory considerations and operational costs.

## Key Concepts

- Federated learning
- Differential privacy
- Secure aggregation
- Model drift and data poisoning
- Rollout strategy and cost modeling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: simulate a DP-guarded update
function dpUpdate(weights, clip, sigma){
  // clip
  let clipped = weights.map(w => Math.max(-clip, Math.min(clip, w)));
  // add noise
  return clipped.map(w => w + randn(0, sigma));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure break-even and what data would you collect during rollout?
- What privacy/regulatory trade-offs would influence DP epsilon targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cost-benefit framework for cross-app, on-device data sharing with strong privacy constraints and a realistic rollout plan.

## Key Concepts

- On-device cross-app data sharing with strict isolation
- User opt-out and privacy/compliance controls
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, energy
- Telemetry delta economics and governance
- Staged rollout and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: anonymize and bucket signals before local merge
function processEvent(event) {
  if (!userOptIn) return;
  const bucket = hash(event.type) % 10;
  buckets[bucket].push(event);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and adapt to schema drift in shared signals?
- What rollback plan and telemetry safeguards would you implement if opt-out is toggled mid-rollout?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model privacy-preserving analytics, not ML, with real-world trade-offs across storage, compute, energy, and compliance.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy budgets & CRDTs for local aggregation
- Edge-resource constraints and telemetry design
- Compliance costs and regulatory penalties

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple CRDT-like Grow-Only Counter
class GCounter { constructor() { this.counters = new Map(); }
inc(k, v=1){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0)+v,0)); }
merge(other){ for(const [k,v] of other.counters){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0), v)); } }
value(){ let s=0; for(const v of this.counters.values()) s+=v; return s; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle CRDT reconciliation when offline and merging after sync?
- What metrics would you track to detect drift in your aggregates and how would you price the analytics feature?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to quantify economics of on-device ML features, balancing privacy, latency, and cloud savings at scale across enterprises.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing and embeddings
- Energy budgets and edge compute
- Delta-sync and privacy-compliant telemetry
- Multi-tenant rollout and org-level scaling
- Regulatory/compliance costs and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple relevant cash-flow function for CBA
function cbaNPV(cf, r) {
  let npv = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t < cf.length; t++) {
    npv += cf[t] / Math.pow(1 + r, t + 1);
  }
  return npv;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the energy model across devices with different hardware?
- How would you quantify productivity uplift from improved search latency in organizations?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a feature into a measurable business and engineering plan, focusing on CBA fundamentals, trade-offs, and governance for a client-side offline feature.

## Key Concepts

- **Client-side indexing and offline user experience**: Implementing lightweight search indexes (e.g., Lunr) for instant offline note search
- **Cost modeling**: Storage, CPU, latency, and privacy considerations
- **Rollout planning**: Phased deployment with validation metrics and maintenance scheduling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple Lunr index search
const index = lunr(function() {
  this.field('title', { boost: 10 });
  this.field('content');
  this.ref('id');
});

documents.forEach(doc => index.add(doc));

const results = index.search('search term');
```

## Why This Is Asked
